---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import FAQSection from '../../components/FAQSection.astro';
import TemplateCallout from '../../components/templates/TemplateCallout.astro';
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
import DemoVideo from '../../components/DemoVideo.astro';
import { config } from '../../config';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is construction change order software?',
    answer:
      'Construction change order software — also called change order management software — is the system a contractor uses to document a scope change, price it, get it approved, and bill it. In BuildWorkPro that workflow is built for subcontractors: each change order is tied to a project, numbered automatically, priced as add/modify/remove line items against the original bid, signed or approved, then rolled into the next pay application. It is not a GC portal for collecting extras from many trades.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a change order in construction?',
    answer:
      'A change order is a written record of a change to a project’s scope and its cost impact — work added, modified, or removed after the contract was signed. Once approved, it adjusts the contract value so the changed work is billable. For subcontractors, it replaces the verbal go-ahead that too often turns into unpaid work.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How are change orders created and priced in BuildWorkPro?',
    answer:
      'You create a change order against a project with a title, a reason — Customer Request, Field Conditions, Design Change, or Other — and a description. It starts in Draft with an automatic number like CO-001, incrementing per project. Then you price it with cost items: each has a change type (Add, Modify, or Remove), a quantity, unit price, labor, and tax, and can reference the bid line it affects. The line amounts roll into the change order total.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What does the approval workflow look like?',
    answer:
      'A change order moves through five statuses: Draft, Submitted, Approved, Rejected, and Void. You cannot submit one without at least one cost item, so there is always a priced record behind every approval. Approving, rejecting, or voiding requires a company admin or manager, and approving adjusts the project’s contract value by the change order’s net amount. Voiding an approved change order reverses its effect on the contract.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do approved change orders affect pay applications?',
    answer:
      'When you create a pay application, the Change Orders Total prefills from the project’s approved change orders, alongside the Original Contract Sum from the accepted bid. The first pay app on a project also seeds its schedule of values from pending approved change orders. Approved scope changes show up in your billing without re-entering any numbers.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does BuildWorkPro produce AIA-style change order documents?',
    answer:
      'Yes. You send the customer a priced change-order PDF with the description, reason, line items, and the contract-sum adjustment — the same information a GC expects on a G701-style form. BuildWorkPro is not a licensed AIA Contract Documents product. If a job requires the official AIA G701, transfer the same numbers onto that form. For a paper workflow, the free construction change order template at buildworkpro.com/templates/change-order/ covers the manual version.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How is change order software different from GC project software?',
    answer:
      'GC platforms are built to receive, log, and approve change orders from many trades on the GC’s job. Change order software for subcontractors is built to create those extras: write the scope change, price it against your bid, get a signature, and bill it on your pay app. Collaborator access on a GC’s project is not a system of record for your company. BuildWorkPro is the second kind.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is there a construction change order app?',
    answer:
      'BuildWorkPro is construction change order software you open in a browser on a phone, tablet, or desktop — so a superintendent can start a CO from the field without installing a separate app. Native iOS and Android apps are not the current product. There is a 14-day free trial with no credit card required, then change orders are included in the $79/month plan.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much does it cost?',
    answer:
      'Change orders are included in the single BuildWorkPro plan: $79/month (or $790/year) with unlimited users, unlimited projects, and every feature. There is a 14-day free trial with no credit card required.',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Construction Change Order Software | BuildWorkPro"
  description="Construction change order software for subcontractors: price scope changes, get approval, and bill them on the next pay app. $79/mo, 14-day free trial."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-16">
      <div class="text-center">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">Features</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">
          Construction Change Order Software for Subcontractors
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-slate-500 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Document the scope change, price it as line items, get it approved, and bill it on the
          next pay app. Construction change order software built for specialty subcontractors — not
          a GC portal for collecting extras from other trades.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-4">
          <a
            href={config.signupUrl}
            class="inline-flex items-center px-8 py-3.5 text-base font-semibold text-white bg-brand-600 hover:bg-brand-700 rounded-xl transition-all hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:shadow-lg"
          >
            Start free trial
          </a>
          <a
            href="/features/"
            class="inline-flex items-center px-8 py-3.5 text-base font-semibold text-slate-700 bg-white hover:bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-xl transition-all"
          >
            All features
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Product proof -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <DemoVideo
        src="/videos/change-order-demo.mp4"
        poster="/videos/change-order-demo-poster.jpg"
        label="Demo: adding a priced mark to a change order — a catalog product built from material and labor sublines, with the contract impact calculated from cost"
        caption="Pricing a change order in BuildWorkPro: add a mark from your catalog, build it from material and labor sublines, and watch the contract impact and projected contract total update as it saves."
      />
    </section>

    <!-- Overview -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-slate mx-auto">
        <h2>A verbal OK is not a change order</h2>
        <p>
          It happens on every job. The GC asks for something outside the original scope, your crew
          does it on a nod, and months later the invoice gets questioned because nothing was written
          down. BuildWorkPro gives that moment a process:
          <a href="/docs/change-orders/creating/">create the change order</a>, price it as line
          items, send the customer a PDF, and record the approval before the work starts.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every change order is tied to a project and numbered automatically — CO-001, CO-002 — so
          there is always a record to point to. Each one carries a reason (customer request, field
          conditions, design change) and a status from Draft through Submitted to Approved or
          Rejected. Cost items can reference the line in the
          <a href="/features/construction-bidding/">original bid</a> they change, and approving a change
          order adjusts the project's contract value by its net amount.
        </p>
        <p>
          Approved means billable: the approved total prefills the Change Orders Total on your next
          <a href="/features/pay-applications/">pay application</a>. Leave a change in Draft or
          Submitted at the end of the job and it will not be on the final pay app — unresolved
          extras are one of the fastest ways retainage gets stuck on the
          <a href="/blog/construction-project-closeout-checklist/">closeout checklist</a>. For the
          wider process — pricing changes, getting sign-off, protecting your margin — read our guide
          on
          <a href="/blog/construction-change-order-management/"
            >construction change order management</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Subcontractor change-order workflow -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-slate mx-auto">
        <h2>The subcontractor change-order workflow</h2>
        <p>
          GC software is built to <em>receive</em> extras. Construction change order software for subcontractors
          is built to <em>create</em> them. The workflow is scope change → price → approval → billing
          — written down before the crew starts, so the extra work is a contract adjustment instead of
          a memory.
        </p>
        <h3>Document the scope change</h3>
        <p>
          The moment the GC directs work outside the original scope, open a change order on the
          project: title, reason (customer request, field conditions, design change, or other), and
          a description of what changed. It starts in Draft with an automatic number like CO-001.
          That record is the notice trail most subcontracts require — not a text that says "we'll
          square up later."
        </p>
        <h3>Price it against the original bid</h3>
        <p>
          Cost items carry a change type — Add, Modify, or Remove — with quantity, unit price,
          labor, and tax. Each line can reference the
          <a href="/features/construction-bidding/">original bid</a> line it affects, so the extra is
          priced the same way the job was estimated. Deductive change orders use the same workflow when
          scope comes out. The total can be positive or negative; the contract always matches what was
          actually approved.
        </p>
        <h3>Get a signature that is the approval</h3>
        <p>
          Submit the change order, email the priced PDF, and optionally send it for
          <a href="/features/change-orders/#e-signatures">e-signature</a>. A signed change order is
          an approved one: the net amount adjusts the project's contract value. Company admins and
          managers can also record Approve or Reject without a signature. You cannot submit a change
          order without at least one cost item — there is always a priced record behind the yes.
        </p>
        <h3>Bill it on the pay application</h3>
        <p>
          Approved construction change orders prefill the Change Orders Total on the next
          <a href="/features/pay-applications/">AIA-style pay application</a>, alongside the
          original contract sum from the accepted bid. The first pay app on a project can also seed
          its schedule of values from pending approved change orders. Changed work goes on the
          invoice, not into a closeout fight.
        </p>
        <h3>AIA-style change order documents</h3>
        <p>
          The PDF your GC gets carries the description, reason, line items, and the running
          contract-sum adjustment — the same information a G701-style change order is supposed to
          show. BuildWorkPro is not a licensed AIA Contract Documents product; if a job requires the
          official G701, transfer the same numbers onto that form. For a paper workflow, start with
          the free
          <a href="/templates/change-order/">construction change order template</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- vs GC-first tools -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 text-center mb-4">
        Not GC change-order software
      </h2>
      <p class="text-slate-500 text-center max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-10 leading-relaxed">
        Most change order management software is built for general contractors who approve extras.
        Specialty subcontractors write, price, and bill them — so the tool has to be a system of
        record for your company, not a guest login on the GC's job.
      </p>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <div class="p-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
            GC-first tools
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-4">Receive and approve extras</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
            <li>Built to collect change orders from many trades on the GC's project.</li>
            <li>
              Your priced extra often still starts in Excel, then gets pasted into their portal.
            </li>
            <li>Collaborator access covers that GC's job — not every customer you work for.</li>
            <li>
              Approved extras live in the GC's budget, not in
              <em>your</em> contract value and next pay app.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-500">
            Fine when a GC requires you to log a CO in their system. That login is not how you get
            paid across every job — see
            <a
              href="/blog/subcontractor-vs-general-contractor-software/"
              class="text-brand-600 hover:underline"
              >subcontractor vs. general contractor software</a
            >.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-8 rounded-2xl border border-brand-200 bg-brand-50/40 ring-1 ring-brand-100">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
            Subcontractor change orders
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-4">
            BuildWorkPro construction change orders
          </h3>
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
            <li>
              You write the extra: reason, description, and line-item pricing against the bid.
            </li>
            <li>Draft → Submitted → Approved, with e-signature as the approval if you want it.</li>
            <li>Approving adjusts <em>your</em> contract value — including deductive COs.</li>
            <li>
              The approved total prefills the next
              <a href="/features/pay-applications/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline"
                >pay application</a
              >. No re-entry.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-500">
            $79/month flat, unlimited users, 14-day free trial. Change orders are included — not a
            separate module.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Key Capabilities -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 text-center mb-12">
        Key capabilities
      </h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-8">
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-amber-50 ring-1 ring-amber-100 mb-4">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-amber-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M12 9v6m3-3H9m12 0a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Every change order, one list</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            See every change order across your projects with its CO number, project, title, reason,
            amount, and status. Filter by status or reason, or open the Change Orders tab inside any
            project.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-amber-50 ring-1 ring-amber-100 mb-4">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-amber-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M12 6v12m-3-2.818l.879.659c1.171.879 3.07.879 4.242 0 1.172-.879 1.172-2.303 0-3.182C13.536 12.219 12.768 12 12 12c-.725 0-1.45-.22-2.003-.659-1.106-.879-1.106-2.303 0-3.182s2.9-.879 4.006 0l.415.33M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Line-item pricing</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            Price each change as cost items with a change type — Add, Modify, or Remove — quantity,
            unit price, labor, and tax. Each line can reference the bid line it affects, and amounts
            roll into the change order total.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-amber-50 ring-1 ring-amber-100 mb-4">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-amber-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M9 12.75L11.25 15 15 9.75M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Approval workflow</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            Change orders move through Draft, Submitted, Approved, Rejected, and Void. A change
            order can't be submitted without at least one cost item, and only company admins and
            managers can approve, reject, or void.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-amber-50 ring-1 ring-amber-100 mb-4">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-amber-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M16.023 9.348h4.992v-.001M2.985 19.644v-4.992m0 0h4.992m-4.993 0l3.181 3.183a8.25 8.25 0 0013.803-3.7M4.031 9.865a8.25 8.25 0 0113.803-3.7l3.181 3.182"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Contract value adjustment</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            Approving a change order adjusts the project's contract value by the net amount of its
            cost items. Voiding an approved change order reverses the adjustment — the contract
            always matches what was actually approved.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-amber-50 ring-1 ring-amber-100 mb-4">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-amber-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M15 12H9m12 0a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Deductive change orders</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            Change order totals can be negative. When scope is removed, a deductive change order
            lowers the contract value through the same documented workflow — no side agreements.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-amber-50 ring-1 ring-amber-100 mb-4">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-amber-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M19.5 14.25v-2.625a3.375 3.375 0 00-3.375-3.375h-1.5A1.125 1.125 0 0113.5 7.125v-1.5a3.375 3.375 0 00-3.375-3.375H8.25m0 12.75h7.5m-7.5 3H12M10.5 2.25H5.625c-.621 0-1.125.504-1.125 1.125v17.25c0 .621.504 1.125 1.125 1.125h12.75c.621 0 1.125-.504 1.125-1.125V11.25a9 9 0 00-9-9z"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Ready for billing</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            Send the change order to your customer as a PDF by email. Once approved, the total
            prefills the Change Orders Total on your next
            <a href="/features/pay-applications/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline"
              >pay application</a
            >.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Approval screenshot -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <Screenshot
        src="/screenshots/25-change-order-approved.png"
        alt="An approved change order in BuildWorkPro after adjusting the project's contract value"
        caption="An approved change order: the project's contract value is adjusted by the net amount, ready to bill on the next pay app."
        width={1280}
        height={720}
      />
    </section>

    <!-- E-signature -->
    <section id="e-signatures" class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20 scroll-mt-32">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white p-8 sm:p-10">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-sky-50 ring-1 ring-sky-100 shrink-0">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-sky-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M16.862 4.487l1.687-1.688a1.875 1.875 0 112.652 2.652L10.582 16.07a4.5 4.5 0 01-1.897 1.13L6 18l.8-2.685a4.5 4.5 0 011.13-1.897l8.932-8.931zm0 0L19.5 7.125M18 14v4.75A2.25 2.25 0 0115.75 21H5.25A2.25 2.25 0 013 18.75V8.25A2.25 2.25 0 015.25 6H10"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">
              A signature that is the approval
            </h2>
            <p class="mt-3 text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
              Send a change order for legally-binding e-signature and stop chasing an email that
              says "approved." Your customer signs from a link — no account, no app, no login — and
              the signature itself authorizes the work.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-8 grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
          <div>
            <div
              class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold mb-3"
            >
              1
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1">Send it for signature</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
              Request a signature when you email the change order. The signing link goes out
              alongside the PDF your customer already expects.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div
              class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold mb-3"
            >
              2
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1">They sign from the link</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
              One link, any browser. While it is out, the change order carries an "Awaiting
              signature" badge — and you can resend or void the request at any time.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div
              class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold mb-3"
            >
              3
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1">The contract updates</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
              A signed change order is an approved one: the net amount adjusts the project's
              contract value and rolls into your next pay application.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
          The certified signed PDF files itself onto the change order's Documents, and every
          signature event lands in the activity feed and the audit log — so the authorization to do
          the work is attached to the work. E-signature runs on
          <a href="/integrations/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Documenso</a> and is included
          in the $79/month plan.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How It Works -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 text-center mb-12">How it works</h2>
      <div class="space-y-8">
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div
            class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold"
          >
            1
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1">Document the change</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-500">
              Create a change order against the project with a title, a reason — Customer Request,
              Field Conditions, Design Change, or Other — and a description. It starts in Draft with
              an automatic number like CO-001.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div
            class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold"
          >
            2
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1">Price it as line items</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-500">
              Add cost items with a change type, quantity, unit price, labor, and tax, referencing
              the original bid line where relevant. The total can be positive or negative — removed
              scope is documented the same way as added scope.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div
            class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold"
          >
            3
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1">Submit and approve</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-500">
              Submit the change order to move it to Submitted, and send your customer the PDF. A
              company admin or manager records the decision — Approve or Reject — and an approval
              adjusts the contract value by the net amount.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div
            class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold"
          >
            4
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1">Bill it on the next pay app</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-500">
              When you create your next pay application, the Change Orders Total prefills from the
              project's approved change orders alongside the original contract sum — the changed
              work goes on the invoice, not into a dispute.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-10 text-center text-sm text-slate-400">
